mediator_coefficients <- data.frame( die=c(1,2),
                                     sphere_coefficients = c(
                                       round(summary(icsme_mediator_initial_temp1)$coefficients["workhot-first",1],2),
                                       round(summary(icsme_mediator_initial_temp2)$coefficients["workhot-first",1],2)
                                       ),
                                     schaffer_coefficients=c(
                                       round(summary(mediator_initial_temp1)$coefficients["workhot-first",1],2),
                                       round(summary(mediator_initial_temp2)$coefficients["workhot-first",1],2)
                                       )
                                     )

mediator_final_temp2 <- lm(final_temp_2 ~ work + dimension * population_size * alpha,
                     data = schaffer_workload_data)

kable( mediator_coefficients, caption="Mediator model coefficients")
@

Since we are interested in reducing the energy consumed by algorithm implementations and how the hot-first intervention affects it, we show in Figure \ref{fig:ieeecal.factors} how the intervention affects energy consumption (baseline is represented by circles while hot-first uses triangles). Since we found in the model that the most important temperature variable is that of the second die, with a coefficient of \Sexpr{round(schaffer_workload_model$coefficients["initial_temp_2"],2)}, we also color points according to this temperature, clamped to the most common 38-44º range. In some cases, we see that the hot-first strategy consumes {\em less} energy, and it does so mainly because the temperature gradient between the two dies is kept, allowing passive heat dissipation and reducing energy consumption. In fact, if we model the initial temperature of the second die using as factors the intervention as well as the algorithm parameters, the coefficient for the intervention is \Sexpr{round(summary(mediator_initial_temp2)$coefficients["workhot-first",1],2)}; more interestingly, the coefficient of the intervention for the {\em final} temperature (taken after every program is finished) is \Sexpr{round(summary(mediator_final_temp2)$coefficients["workhot-first",1],2)}. All the coefficients for the initial temperatures for both dies and the two experiments are shown in Table \ref{tab:ieeecal.factors}, exemplifying the different effect of the intervention on both dies, although always lowering the initial temperature and thus creating the conditions for consuming less energy and boosting the signal-to-noise ratio of the actual algorithm parameters.
%
<<ieecal.contrast, echo=F, message=F>>=
# Contrast
#

schaffer_comparison <- ci_comparison(schaffer_workload_data)

kable(schaffer_comparison$comparison, caption="Comparison of confidence intervals, Schaffer experiment.")

ci_ttest <- t.test(schaffer_comparison$test$improvement)
@
%
Besides, in this case, as shown in Table \ref{tab:ieecal.contrast}, the width of the confidence interval is smaller, and the number of possible comparisons that we can perform reliably has increased from 16 to 25 out of 36 possible pairs, a 56\% improvement. This achieves our second objective, which was based on creating a measurement protocol that could be used to compare algorithm configurations more reliably. This reduction is consistent across all contrasts with a 95\% CI of [\Sexpr{round(ci_ttest$conf.int[1], 2)},\Sexpr{round(ci_ttest$conf.int[2], 2)}]. Data for these experiments and source for this paper, including all code for data processing, are available (with a free license) from \url{https://github.com/JJ/brave-new-green-algorithm}.

\section{Conclusion and discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Evolving software towards more energy-efficient versions requires a sound methodology that can differentiate energy spent by different configurations of software under study from the operating context. What we have introduced here is a temperature-aware experimental protocol for measuring the energy consumption of different algorithm implementation configurations for multi-die CPUs that is able to reduce the influence of the operating context, maximizing the possibility of distinguishing between the energy consumption requirements of these configurations. By using temperature-aware process scheduling that always hits the hottest die in a multi-die architecture, we have managed to reduce the temperature and thus the energy consumption for different configurations of our population-based optimization algorithm for two different workloads with different kernel versions, showing that this intervention reduces the overall temperature of the two dies, thus reducing thermal leakage and overall energy consumption. Besides, in an experiment using the heavier workload (Schaffer's function) and more possible configurations, we have shown that the confidence interval of energy measurements is shrunk, allowing reliable comparison for a greater amount of all possible configurations measured.


